# 1.3 Audio hardware design

Read this reference for projects involving microphones, speakers, codecs, audio amplifiers, PDM, I²S, TDM, or other audio interfaces.

It adds audio-specific electrical, acoustic, and mechanical constraints. Use the normal stage references for the schematic, PCB, and bring-up work itself.

## Define the signal path

Draw the actual audio path first, for example:

```text
Microphone
  → analog or digital interface
  → controller or audio processor
  → codec or amplifier
  → speaker
```

State as applicable:

- what is processed on the board;
- what is processed by a host or another device;
- who controls input, output, gain, and mute;
- the output state during power-up, reset, disconnect, and before controller initialization;
- how each major boundary can be measured or observed.

Do not duplicate uncoordinated gain control, resampling, or echo cancellation across parts, devices, or software layers.

For full-duplex playback, barge-in, or acoustic echo cancellation, define the reference source. Audible speaker output does not by itself provide a digital reference to the algorithm.

## Define audio interfaces

For each PDM, I²S, TDM, PCM, or similar interface, record only what the design needs:

- master and slave roles;
- clock source;
- sample rate and bit depth;
- slot width and channel order;
- clock edge, polarity, and left/right selection;
- interface voltage;
- startup, mute, and stop sequence.

Confirm that processor pins support the required peripheral and check:

- pin mux;
- startup and strap restrictions;
- other peripherals used at the same time;
- voltage compatibility;
- whether the required clocks remain available in the target mode.

Put these facts in the schematic Contract and keep them aligned with firmware pins and audio format.

When pins, clocks, or channel format change, update only the affected design facts, firmware interface, and test conditions.

## Handle analog audio paths

For analog microphones, line input, headphones, or analog amplifiers, confirm:

- input and output level range;
- biasing and common-mode range;
- input and load impedance;
- gain distribution;
- AC coupling and low-frequency cutoff;
- DC offset, mute, and startup transients;
- external networks required by the datasheet;
- overvoltage, ESD, or misconnection protection.

Avoid unnecessary gain across several stages. Keep high-gain and low-level nodes away from switching nodes, digital clocks, antennas, and speaker-current paths.

## Handle power and noise

Estimate quiescent, normal, peak or step-load, startup, and fault or maximum-output current for microphones, codecs, controllers, radios, and amplifiers.

Check in particular:

- whether amplifier peaks pull down other rails;
- whether regulators and local decoupling fit the real load step;
- whether microphone and analog supplies share harmful impedance with switching, RF, or amplifier returns;
- whether speaker current and low-level audio share a significant return path;
- whether the amplifier remains muted or safe before controller initialization;
- whether startup or shutdown can create pops or unintended output.

Do not infer source capacity from connector type alone.

Design power and ground regions around actual current loops. Do not split the ground plane mechanically merely because the circuit contains analog audio.

## Handle acoustic and mechanical conditions

Microphone and speaker performance is not determined by the schematic alone.

For microphones, confirm:

- port location and orientation;
- recommended land pattern and opening;
- seal path to the enclosure port;
- no-route, no-copper, or no-via area required by the datasheet;
- spacing and orientation actually required between microphones;
- whether enclosure, adhesive, foam, or waterproofing blocks the sound path.

For speakers, confirm:

- model, impedance, and rated power;
- mounting, retention, and sealing;
- enclosure volume and acoustic outlet;
- vibration coupling into the microphone or PCB;
- connector, harness, and retention under actual current and motion;
- structural resonance or abnormal noise at maximum level.

Define acoustic and RF antenna keepouts separately. They may overlap but do not serve the same purpose merely because both appear empty on the canvas.

## PCB notes

Place and route from datasheets and actual current paths:

- microphone decoupling and filters close to the part;
- codec analog and digital decoupling as specified;
- amplifier bypass, output network, and energy storage close to the amplifier;
- short speaker-current paths away from microphones, analog inputs, and sensitive clocks;
- continuous return reference for PDM, I²S, and other digital audio;
- switching nodes, antennas, amplifier outputs, and speaker magnetic fields away from low-level audio;
- the required acoustic opening and surrounding keepout;
- accessible test points for clocks, data, mute, and power.

Use the normal PCB stage references for implementation.

## Prototype validation

Choose tests from the product's actual function rather than a universal audio checklist. Common tests include:

- interface clocks, data format, and channel order;
- input and output gain;
- microphone noise floor, sensitivity, and unit consistency;
- speaker power, distortion, and temperature rise;
- pops during power-up, reset, mute, and recovery;
- dropped frames or artifacts during radio or processor load;
- playback-to-microphone coupling;
- full duplex, barge-in, or AEC performance;
- rail droop and structural resonance at maximum level;
- behavior before and after enclosure assembly.

Record sample rate, gain, load, volume, power, and enclosure state so results from different conditions are not compared directly.

When a combined path is unfamiliar or depends heavily on software, clocks, enclosure, and acoustics, a development board or module may prove a minimal measurable path first.

A personal prototype may proceed without that proof when the unknown behavior becomes an explicit prototype test. Do not create an extra gate state.

Enter CURIOUS_MODE only for an affected clock-compatibility, acoustic-coupling, temperature, pop-noise, or end-to-end path question that the datasheets do not answer. Return to DEFAULT_MODE once it is resolved or converted into a precise test.
